# Cloudflare Workers Static Assets reads this file at deploy time. Both deploy
# paths send its text as the asset configuration and leave it out of the
# uploaded asset list, so it is never served as a page: wrangler
# (`alepha platform up`) always did, and `lore apps deploy`, which docs uses,
# does since epic #E49. GitHub Pages ignores it entirely.
#
# It exists because Workers Static Assets defaults every asset to
# `cache-control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`. That is right for HTML -
# a docs page must not be stale - and wrong for everything whose URL contains
# its own content hash, where revalidating asks "has the immutable file
# changed?" once per page load, forever.
#
# Every rule that matches a path applies, in file order, and a second rule
# setting the same header is comma-JOINED to the first, not substituted. So a
# rule that overrides an earlier one starts with `! Name`.

# HSTS is the one security header worth setting here: `alepha.dev` is the
# registrable domain, so `includeSubDomains` covers every app under it. The rest
# (CSP, frame-options) would guard against nothing on a site with no auth, no
# cookies and no forms. `nosniff` is free, so it goes in anyway.
#
# Deliberately no `preload` directive: that submits the apex to the browsers'
# built-in HSTS list, which is slow to leave and would strand any subdomain that
# ever needs plain HTTP.
/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=15552000; includeSubDomains
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

# Not content-hashed, so not immutable - but a logo that changes twice a year
# has no business being revalidated on every navigation. A day is the honest
# trade between staleness and traffic.
/*.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/*.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/*.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# The Bay installer. Anyone sensible reads a script before piping it into a
# root shell, so it is pinned to `text/plain` rather than left to whatever a
# `.sh` extension resolves to - browsers download that instead of showing it.
#
# Deliberately no cache rule: the default `max-age=0, must-revalidate` is
# exactly right for an installer, so a fix to it is picked up on the next run
# rather than a day later.
/bay/install.sh
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

# Vite writes the content hash into the name, so these URLs can never change
# meaning. Fonts carry no hash but are equally frozen: the file behind
# `asset.<hash>.woff2` is the hash.
#
# Last, and each removing `Cache-Control` first: a hashed image such as
# `/asset.<hash>.png` also matches the one-day rules above, and without the
# `!` it would answer both values joined, which a cache may read as stale.
/entry.*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/chunk.*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/asset.*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
